│  Penetration test summary                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  # Executive Summary                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  An authorized white-box assessment of the **test-vuln-hard** Flask          │
│  application (a single-file service with three routes backed by a local      │
│  SQLite database) confirmed three independent, unauthenticated injection     │
│  vulnerabilities. Every one of the three ways the application accepts user   │
│  input leads directly to a dangerous sink with no validation, binding, or    │
│  escaping in between.                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  **Overall risk posture:** Critical. The application has no functioning      │
│  security control on any of its input paths. Each of its three endpoints is  │
│  exploitable by an unauthenticated remote attacker.                          │
│                                                                              │
│  **Key findings**                                                            │
│  - **Remote code execution** via server-side template injection on `POST     │
│  /render` (Critical, CVSS 9.8). The attacker controls template source,       │
│  escaped to the Python runtime, ran operating-system commands as the         │
│  application user, and read arbitrary files.                                 │
│  - **SQL injection** in the `GET /orders` sort parameter (High, CVSS 7.5),   │
│  where the value is concatenated into an `ORDER BY` clause. The tester       │
│  recovered a hidden database value one character at a time.                  │
│  - **SQL injection** in the `POST /orders/by-sku` SKU field (High, CVSS      │
│  7.5), allowing `UNION`-based reads of any column and the full database      │
│  schema in a single request.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  **Business impact**                                                         │
│  - **Complete loss of confidentiality and integrity on the host:** the       │
│  code-execution finding gives an unauthenticated party the run of the        │
│  server process, including all data it can reach.                            │
│  - **Full database disclosure:** both SQL paths read arbitrary tables, not   │
│  just the rows the endpoints were built to return, including a value the     │
│  application never displays.                                                 │
│  - **No compensating controls:** there is no authentication, authorization,  │
│  network allowlist, WAF, or sandbox anywhere in the request path, so a       │
│  single unauthenticated request is sufficient for each finding.              │
│                                                                              │
│  **Overarching remediation theme:** every input is being treated as          │
│  executable syntax — a SQL fragment in two places and template source in a   │
│  third. The fix is uniform and small: express user data as *data* (bound     │
│  parameters, a fixed template with values passed as context, an allowlist    │
│  for the one identifier that cannot be bound). All three changes are         │
│  low-effort and independent of each other.                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  # Methodology                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  **Framework:** OWASP Web Security Testing Guide (WSTG) and OWASP Top 10     │
│  categories for injection (A03), plus a white-box source review of the       │
│  application.                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  **Engagement type:** White-box. The complete application source and         │
│  database schema were available, and the service was exercised live from a   │
│  running instance for dynamic confirmation.                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  **Scope:** the `test-vuln-hard` application — `app.py` (three route         │
│  handlers: `GET /orders`, `POST /orders/by-sku`, `POST /render`) and its     │
│  SQLite database `orders.db` (table `orders(id, sku, qty, note)`).           │
│                                                                              │
│  **Approach**                                                                │
│  - **Threat modelling:** the attacker was modelled as an unauthenticated     │
│  party with network reach to the service. No authentication, session,        │
│  tenant, or role model exists, so there is no privilege boundary between an  │
│  anonymous caller and any application function.                              │
│  - **Source-driven triage:** all request inputs were traced to their sinks.  │
│  Three flows were identified — two concatenating input into SQL statement    │
│  text and one passing input to the template engine as template source.       │
│  - **Dynamic validation:** a threat model was agreed, then two specialists   │
│  worked in parallel and independently: one on the SQL sinks, one on the      │
│  template sink. Each produced a working proof of concept against a live      │
│  instance and captured the request/response pairs plus benign baselines.     │
│  - **Counterevidence:** each finding was argued against before filing        │
│  (auto-escaping, error handling, statement stacking limits).                 │
│                                                                              │
│  **Constraint:** the source directory itself is read-only, so the live       │
│  instance was run from an identical copy against its own copy of the         │
│  database. This affects neither the vulnerable source nor the reachability   │
│  of any sink.                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  # Technical Analysis                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  **Severity model:** exploitability multiplied by impact, rated on CVSS      │
│  v3.1. All three findings require no credentials and no user interaction.    │
│                                                                              │
│  1. **Server-side template injection to remote code execution — `POST        │
│  /render`** (Critical, CVSS 9.8, CWE-1336). The handler reads the `tpl`      │
│  form field and passes it directly to `render_template_string`, so user      │
│  input becomes template *source* rather than data. Confirmed by evaluating   │
│  arithmetic (`{{7*7}}` returned `49`, versus the literal string echoed for   │
│  benign input), then by reaching the framework object graph to execute       │
│  operating-system commands (`id`, `uname -a`) and read `/etc/passwd`. The    │
│  module docstring describes the engine as Mako; the import is in fact        │
│  Flask's Jinja2 integration, and the class of defect is identical either     │
│  way. Jinja2's output auto-escaping does not mitigate this — it escapes      │
│  rendered *results*, not the template source that is compiled.               │
│                                                                              │
│  2. **SQL injection in an `ORDER BY` clause — `GET /orders`** (High, CVSS    │
│  7.5, CWE-89). The `sort` parameter is interpolated into the statement text  │
│  at the identifier position, where parameters cannot be bound. Confirmed by  │
│  a conditional expression that reordered results based on a true/false       │
│  predicate, by triggering a database syntax error from a trailing comma, by  │
│  correlated subqueries, and finally by a character-by-character binary       │
│  search that recovered the hidden `note` value for the first row — a column  │
│  this endpoint never returns.                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  3. **`UNION`-based SQL injection — `POST /orders/by-sku`** (High, CVSS      │
│  7.5, CWE-89). The JSON `sku` value is interpolated into a single-quoted     │
│  literal. A tautology returned every row; a three-column `UNION` returned    │
│  the `note` column in-band; and a further `UNION` against the schema table   │
│  returned the full database definition in one response.                      │
│                                                                              │
│  **Systemic theme:** the same defect three times over — attacker input       │
│  treated as executable syntax. There is no shared input-handling or          │
│  query-building layer, no allowlist, and no template sandbox, so each sink   │
│  is independently exploitable and each must be fixed independently.          │
│                                                                              │
│  **Impact bound:** the installed SQLite driver executes one statement per    │
│  call, so neither SQL finding supports stacked statements or writes; their   │
│  demonstrated impact is read-only disclosure. The template injection is not  │
│  similarly bounded and did reach code execution.                             │
│                                                                              │
│  **Relationship between findings:** they share root cause in spirit but not  │
│  in code. The code-execution primitive can also read the database file       │
│  directly, so it subsumes the SQL findings in impact; it does not replace    │
│  them, because each is separately reachable and separately fixable. No       │
│  privilege escalation chain beyond the demonstrated code execution was       │
│  found.                                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  # Recommendations                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  **Immediate**                                                               │
│  1. **Remove template source control at `POST /render`.** Render a fixed,    │
│  developer-authored template and pass the user value as a context variable   │
│  so the engine only ever escapes it as data. Do not rely on a sandboxed      │
│  environment alone; the sink should not accept template source at all.       │
│  2. **Parameterize the `POST /orders/by-sku` query.** Replace the            │
│  interpolated literal with a bound placeholder and pass the value from the   │
│  request body. This eliminates the finding completely with no behavioural    │
│  change for legitimate input.                                                │
│  3. **Allowlist the `GET /orders` sort column.** The identifier position     │
│  cannot be bound, so map the accepted sort keys to a fixed internal list     │
│  and reject anything outside it. Never interpolate a caller-supplied string  │
│  into clause or identifier position.                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  **Short-term**                                                              │
│  4. **Add a regression test per sink** that sends a known injection payload  │
│  and asserts it is treated as data — the executable-payload cases confirmed  │
│  here are a ready-made corpus.                                               │
│  5. **Bound the process's blast radius.** The code-execution finding ran     │
│  with substantial local privileges; run the service as an unprivileged       │
│  account with no elevated group membership, restrict its filesystem reach,   │
│  and ensure no secret keys or credentials are readable from the process      │
│  environment.                                                                │
│  6. **Review the operational log.** The request log is large and records     │
│  every attempted payload; confirm it is rotated, access-restricted, and      │
│  does not retain sensitive request content.                                  │
│                                                                              │
│  **Medium-term**                                                             │
│  7. **Establish a single data-access layer** for database queries so         │
│  statements are constructed in one reviewed place, and use a template        │
│  engine configuration for the application that does not accept dynamic       │
│  source.                                                                     │
│  8. **Add authentication and network restriction** appropriate to the        │
│  service's intended use, so an unauthenticated caller cannot reach any       │
│  handler.                                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  **Retest and validation:** re-run each of the three captured exploit cases  │
│  against the patched build and confirm the payloads return inert data,       │
│  while confirming that legitimate sort keys, SKU lookups, and benign         │
│  template text still behave as before. These three fixes are independent,    │
│  so each can be retested on its own.                                         │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
